Negative control — `setDefault`'s addressability PRECONDITION (AC-13, fix 8a,
parity row L1, ruling R8a).

Reverts `setDefault`'s precondition to the pre-conversion INVERTED guard: the
call to the shared `isAddressable` predicate is replaced by the original
`isAuthenticated.value || !clientId.value`, which resolves TRUE for an
unauthenticated session with no client id and lets the PUT go out at
`clients/undefined/addresses/{id}`.

Declared AC — AC-13: "setDefault issues NO request for an unauthenticated
session with no client id". As AC-11, against `setDefault`: the read-back
asserts an EMPTY request-capture log, not that a rejection surfaced.

No collateral measured, and specifically NOT AC-12. AC-12's happy path — one
`PUT clients/<clientId>/addresses/<id>` with body exactly `{ default: true }`,
then an invalidation — runs on an AUTHENTICATED session, which the inverted
guard still admits, so it stays GREEN here. That is the point of the split: a
red under this mutant means the unauthenticated case stopped being refused, and
nothing else.

Sibling controls: `client-address.auth-guard.must-fail.patch` does the same to
`remove` and declares AC-11; `client-address.client-id-limb.must-fail.patch`
attacks the shared predicate itself and declares AC-3.

